I have seen discussion with integrators fairly recently, where they say the best practice is still to have a renderer method. There are still certian sorts of overriding of the output which is easier to do in a rendere method than by providing a different template. However, with templates, the renderer method should be be pretty minimal: The code that invokes the renderer will look like: 
```text
$output = $PAGE->get_renderer('type_myplugin');


echo $output->render($renderablething);
```
 $renderablething will be an instance of a class in your plugin that implements the renderable and templateable interfaces. Let us suppose it is a \type_myplugin\renderable_thing. That will end up calling the render_renderable_thing method of your renderer: 
```text
// In .../classes/output/renderer.php.


namespace type_myplugin\output;


class renderer extends \plugin_renderer_base {


    public function render_renderable_thing(


            \type_myplugin\renderable_thing $thing) {


        $data = $thing->export_for_template($this);


        return $this->render_from_template(


                'type_myplugin/renderable_thing', $data);


    }


}
```
 As I say, the only reason to do this is to make certain sorts of output rendering easier. Up to you whether that is worth it is your case, but it is not actually a lot of work once you get the hang of it. (We have been writing most recent ou-specific plugins this way. As I say, the last I heard, this is considered good practice for new features going into Moodle core.

