Hi Richard, the thing is Moodle uses it's own session storage using a different path than the standard PHP sessions. So we need to tell PHP where to look things for. In addition to that, we need to make sure Moodle doesn't use the database for session storage, but the file system instead (otherwise things are a bit more complicated). So you need to set your Moodle PHP session settings as shown in the next picture (the timeout value is not that important, the 'Use database for session information' and 'Cookie path' settings are the ones you need to take care of): Once you have those settings in place, you can use something like the following code to get access to Moodle session variables. WARNING: the code is very simple and destroys your existing session and replaces it with Moodle's one. In real life you will probably need to keep your own session and get some addition Moodle session data, so you will need to add additional code to handle that scenario. Remember we can't simply 'require_once' config.php from Moodle, as it does a lot of things we don't want to. But we need some information from config.php, namely the path where Moodle stores its own session files. So we need to supply that value by hand. And pretty much the same for the session name. Here I've used $CFG to hold that configuration values. 
```text
<?php




$CFG = new stdClass();


$CFG->
dataroot
 = '/usr/src/moodle/moodledata';


$CFG->cookiename = 'MoodleSession';
```
