What you are seeing is a timezone issue. FROM_UNIXTIME uses the MySQL timezone (whatever that may be) when doing the conversion. In order to get the desired timezone you can do these things: 
```text
global $DB;


$sessiontz = $DB->get_field_sql('SELECT @@session.time_zone');


$dt = new DateTime('now', \core_date::get_user_timezone_object());


$tzdiff = $dt->getOffset();


$tzhours = (int)($tzdiff / HOURSECS);


$tzmins = (int)(($tzdiff % HOURSECS) / MINSECS);


$res = sprintf("%+'03d:%'02d", $tzhours, abs($tzmins));


$DB->execute("SET SESSION time_zone='{$res}'");


// Execute here your query!


$records = $DB->get_records_sql($sql, $params);


// Do stuff.


// Revert to original session timezone.


$DB->execute("SET SESSION time_zone='{$sessiontz}'");
```
 The above code get's the mysql session timezone, stores it, determines the moodle timezone and configures current db session to use that timezone. Than you execute your query do whatever you need to do and finally set session to original value.

Basic principals of object orientated programming: 
```text
class bob {


    public function say_hello() {


        echo "Hello my name is ".$this->get_name();


    }


    protected function get_name() {


        return 'Bob';


    }


}


class sam extends class bob {


    protected function get_name() {


        return 'Sam';


   }


}


$inst = new bob();


$inst2 = new sam();




$inst->say_hello();  // This will print out 'Hello my name is Bob';


$inst2->say_hello(); // This will print out 'Hello my name is Sam';
```
 In this case, the sub class 'sam' has inherited the 'say_hello' function from the base class 'bob', but it has overridden the function 'get_name', so that, instead of returning 'Bob' (as it does when you use the base class 'bob') it now returns 'Sam'.

