Renderer for a plugin must be in the [my plugin dir]/renderer.php . The class inside must be named [plugin component]_renderer and must extend plugin_renderer_base. As for - "should I use renderables or not?" That really depends. The idea behind renderables is to provide a way to organize complex data structures that can be passed as single parameter to renderer method. To provide silly example: 
```text
class local_myextraplugin_data implements renderable {


    public $param1 = '';


    public $param2 = '';


    public $param3 = '';


    public $param4 = '';


    public $param5 = '';


    public $param6 = '';




    public function __construct() {


        $this->param1 = 'a';


        $this->param2 = 'b';


        $this->param3 = 'c';


        $this->param4 = 'd';


        $this->param5 = 'e';


        $this->param6 = 'f';


    }


}




class local_myextraplugin_renderer extends plugin_renderer_base {


    public function renderthis($param1, $param2, $param3, $param4, $param5, $param6) {


        $result = sprintf('%s %s %s %s %s %s', $param1, $param2, $param3, $param4, $param5, $param6);


        return $result;


    }




    public function renderthat(local_myextraplugin_data $data) {


        $result = sprintf('%s %s %s %s %s %s', $data->param1, $data->param2, $data->param3, $data->param4, $data->param5, $data->param6);


        return $result;


    }


}
```
 Without renderable we have a renderer method with 6 input parameters. With renderable object parameter is just one. This is pure organizational convenience. You can use it or not depending on your specific case and preference.

