I've had a similar problem (using Moodle 2.7) and found a solution that might help you as well. Events such as document.ready won't work, because the editor is initialized through JavaScript code after the document is loaded. As it turns out, when the editor is ready it sends an event ('pluginsloaded'), but that event is published locally - only to the objects inside the editor itself. For other purposes I have already duplicated one of the editor's plugin (to avoid core changes) so it was relatively easy from there on. In the plugin's initializer I've added the following lines: 
```text
Y.publish('editorReady', {


    emitFacade: true,


    broadcast: 2


});


this.get('
host
').on('pluginsloaded', function(e) {


    Y.fire('editorReady', {


        toolbar: e.target.toolbar


    });


});
```
 This way, the moment the editor is ready, a custom event is sent. Then, on a JavaScript file in the theme, I added this part 
```text
Y.Global.on('editorReady', function(e) {


    ...


});
```
 I needed to handle each editor separately so I've added the toolbar object to the event. Otherwise there's no need for it. The downsize for you is that YUI and JQUERY events don't mix. I wish they had.

