Hi Neeraj, here there is a quick example of how you should proceed using moodle_database facilities to access to your external database. The main advantage is that moodle_database wraps any of the supported drivers (mysqli, pgsql, mssql, sqlsrv, oci8) and tomorrow, if you move your server from SQL*Server to, say, PostgreSQL the change will be trivial (just connect to another database). Note that many of the methods in that API are not suitable for external (non-moodle) databases so, in the example below I've shown the main 2 you should be using, one for fetching information and the other for the rest of operations. To try it, just put it in the main directory of a Moodle site, edit it to adjust the connection properties to your "external" SQL*Server, and execute it from command line with `php connect_to_external_db.php` . 
```text
<?php
// Only executable from PHP Cli. Not suitable from web (unprotected).
// Any error will throw exceptions. Can be caught if needed.
define('CLI_SCRIPT', true);

require('config.php');

// Get our prefered database driver (mysqli, pgsql, mssql, sqlsrv, oci8).
// Last parameter (external = true) means we are not connecting to a Moodle database.
$mydb = moodle_database::get_driver_instance('sqlsrv', 'native', true);

// Connection properties.
$
host
 = '1.2.3.4';
$user = 'username';
$pass = 'passs';
$database = 'databasename';
$prefix = '';

// Connect.
$mydb->connect($host, $user, $pass, $database, $prefix);

echo "Connected!\n";

// Let's try it, with a query to SQL*Server well known table.
// For queries, get_recordset_sql() is recommended, it
// supports placeholders, limit records...
$sql = 'SELECT table_name AS name FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES';
$rs = $mydb->get_recordset_sql($sql);
foreach ($rs as $table) {
    echo " - Found table {$table->name}\n";
}
$rs->close();

// In general any SQL statement can be executed with execute()
// (and it accepts placeholders too). Better use this instead of
// moodle_database insert/update/delete methods.
// Of course note the next insert will fail because that table
// does not exist inyour server, so I've commented the ->execute() call.
$sql = 'INSERT INTO some_table_in_your_server (name, value) VALUES (:aname, :avalue)';
$params = array('aname' => 'names', 'avalue' => 'matter!');
// $mydb->execute($sql, $params);

echo "Record inserted\n";

// When we do not need it any more.
$mydb->dispose();

echo "Disconected!\n";
```
