It would be nice to revisit the Coding Guidelines for developers, specifically to implement a coding guideline for CSS .  Currently the only guidelines for CSS are that it "should be used for layout".  The problem is that a theme can use thousands of lines of CSS, and when inherited from a parent it can be very difficult to traverse. A single file might have something like: 
```text
#id {rule:declaration;}


#another_id { rule: declaration; }


#anotherId {


    rule: declaration;


}


.class, .second-class {rule: declaration;anotherrule:declaration;}


.anotherclass,


.thirdclass {


    rule: declaration;


    secondrule: declaration;


}
```
 This can obviously make things very difficult to traverse and "debug".  Just to be clear, I'm not talking about implementing a 'best-practices' guideline, but a coding guideline.  Here's some reading I've been doing lately: Writing CSS for Others CSSdoc Draft Standard WordPress CSS Coding Standards H5BP Docs Beyond this basic framework, a Moodle specific guideline would be nice that would deal with things like: inline includes, post-processing, file naming, @media declarations, handling browser hacks, etc...

