The open2study portal is pretty, and most of the basic features of the design could be tackled in the theme pretty easily. Without knowing a bit of a plan of the things you want to replicate it's hard to suggest many changes. However, it sound like you want to customise the functionality of various pages moreso than the look. To a degree this is possible with custom renderers. In your theme's config.php file, enable the theme overridden renderer - 
```text
$THEME->rendererfactory = 'theme_overridden_renderer_factory';
```
 and then create / modify the theme's renderers.php file, and then override core_xxx_renderer, where xxx is something like "course". E.g. 
```text
class theme_adventuretime_core_course_renderer extends core_course_renderer {


   public function course_category($category) {


        global $CFG;


        require_once($CFG->libdir. '/coursecatlib.php');


        $coursecat = coursecat::get(is_object($category) ? $category->id : $category);


        ... etc


    }


}


class theme_aspen_core_renderer extends core_renderer {


    protected function render_custom_menu(custom_menu $menu) {


    .... etc
```
 Basically you take a core renderer and replace it with your own. This way you can take a function that is doing something deep in moodle (say rendering the course category drop down or search bar on a catalogue) and replacing it with functionality that you want. A more extreme way to modify moodle is to override files using customscripts, which can be helpful if you can't override a renderer or need to modify a form. This is forgotten feature of Moodle that lets you take a moodle file and replace it with your own - so that when moodle requests a file - say /course/edit_form.php - it actually executes whatever file you replaced it with, as if it were in its place. Enable this by modifing the /config.php file (in the moodle folder), and adding: 
```text
    $CFG->customscripts = __DIR__.'/local/customscripts';
```
 (or wherever you store them, I like to use the /local/ folder). Inside this folder you replicate the moodle path to the file - say "./courses/edit.php" and then when moodle goes to load that path, it loads your file instead. This is handy if you've got a custom editor, but can just as easily be used to replace the course category page, or the scorm player page, or whatever you like. If you post back with a brief of what you are thinking of modifying, perhaps I can be more specific. Cheers, Tim

