you can do either.  say there's a class like this: 
```text
class core_renderer {



  function helloworld() {



    return "foo";



  }



}
```
 and a theme has extended the class like this 
```text
theme_parent_core_renderer extends core_renderer {



  function helloworld() {



    return "foobar";



  }



}
```
 Then you have a child theme, you can decide to extend either one. 
```text
theme_child_core_renderer extends core_renderer {



  function helloworld() {



    return "hello " . "foo"; 


  }



}
```
 The output chain would look like core_renderer -> theme_child_core_renderer. The resulting code will be 
```text
hello foo
```
 completely bypassing what the parent_theme did to the class. But if you extend the parent_theme method you will start with it's extensions and extend from there: 
```text
theme_child_core_renderer extends theme_parent_core_renderer {



  function helloworld() {



    return "hello " . "foobar"; 


  }



}
```
 So the chain looks like core_renderer -> theme_parent_core_renderer -> theme_child_core_renderer. The resulting output would be 
```text
hello foobar
```
 . obviously in my examples I'm completely blowing away the methods- but it's just for a short demo, you can properly extend.

