The difference between get_records_* and get_recordset_* is that get_records_* loads all the results into memory and get_recordset_* does not. So for example, these two will produce the same result: 
```text
$users = $DB->get_records('user');


foreach ($users as $user) {


    echo fullname($user).'<br/>';


}
```

```text
$users = $DB->get_recordset('user');


foreach ($users as $user) {


    echo fullname($user).'<br/>';


}
```
 But the first will use more memory (assuming there are several users on the site). However, with the first one, you have an array, which you can use in all the standard PHP array functions (e.g. array_keys(), count(), array_slice(), etc.), whereas the second returns an iterable object you can loop through, but not use array functions on. The second is particularly helpful if you are, for example, exporting details of all the users on the site as a CSV or wanting to perform some sort of upgrade on every activity on the site (so loading all of them into memory could cause issues). For most other purposes, get_records_* is usually OK.

